{{also|Potash}}
==English==
{{wikipedia|lang=en}}

===Etymology===
{{calque|en|nl|potas}}, coined in 1598, equivalent to {{compound|en|pot|ash}}. The literal translation is “[[pot]] [[ash]]”, because it was made by burning wood to ashes in a large pot. First attested in 1648. {{doublet|en|potassium|potassa}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈpɒt.æʃ/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈpɑt.æʃ/}}
* {{rhymes|en|æʃ}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-potash.wav|Audio (UK)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The [[water]]-[[soluble]] part of the [[ash]] formed by [[burning]] [[plant]] [[material]]; used for making [[soap]] and [[glass]] and as a [[fertilizer]].
# {{lb|en|chemistry}} An [[impure]] form of [[potassium]] [[carbonate]] (K<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub>) mixed with other potassium [[salt]]s.
# {{lb|en|chemistry|archaic}} {{l|en|potassium|Potassium}}. {{n-g|Chiefly used in the names of [[compound]]s of the form "... of potash".}}
#: {{uxi|en|[[permanganate of potash]]|[[potassium permanganate]]}}
# A class of [[potassium]] minerals, of similar applicability to [[potassium carbonate]], such as being a fertilizer. (ie. [[potassium chloride]], [[potassium hydroxide]])

